course: dictionary
lesson: 3195
title: Endorsement
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An endorsement is public support or approval. When someone says «yes, I back this», they give an endorsement. You can give one to a person, a plan or a product. News articles and adverts use the word a lot, so it helps to know it.
explain: Read the context. «An endorsement is when a famous person promotes a product, often paid a lot, maybe £1 million; it does not prove they really use it.» The words paid and promotes are the clues. A famous person is being paid to recommend something, so endorsement must mean a paid public recommendation.
explain: Look for the phrases too: «endorsement deal», «a strong endorsement» and «celebrity endorsement». In an election, a famous person may give an endorsement to a candidate, which tells readers that the person supports them. Ask yourself: who is saying yes, and what are they saying yes to?
fact: An endorsement is public support or approval for a person, plan or product.
fact: In adverts, an endorsement deal means a famous person is paid to say they use or like a product.
fact: A «celebrity endorsement» does not always mean the celebrity really uses the product.
fact: In the UK, an endorsement on a driving licence is a note showing the driver broke a traffic law.
fact: An endorsement can also be a signature on the back of a cheque. Context tells you which meaning is used.
exercise: «An endorsement is when a famous person promotes a product, often paid a lot.» What does endorsement mean here? | *A famous person publicly backing a product, often for money | A free holiday given to a famous person | A law that bans adverts for food | A note on a driving licence
exercise: The footballer signed an endorsement deal with a sportswear company. What is an endorsement deal? | A deal where the footballer buys a new car | *A deal where the footballer is paid to promote the company's clothes | A contract to stop playing football | A football match against the company
exercise: In the election, the actor gave a strong endorsement to one candidate. What does this tell the reader? | That the actor is standing for election himself | That the actor has lost his voice | That the actor is angry about the election | *That the actor publicly said he supports that candidate
exercise: Which word in «An endorsement is when a famous person promotes a product» tells you the person is recommending something? | product | *promotes | endorsement | famous
test: «It does not prove they really use it.» What does this tell you about a celebrity endorsement? | The celebrity uses the product every single day | The product is always the best on the market | The celebrity has to give the product back | *The celebrity may be paid to say they like a product, even if they never use it
test: A driver got an endorsement on her licence for breaking a traffic law. What does endorsement mean here? | *A note showing she broke a traffic law | A brand new driving licence | A prize for driving safely for years | A signature on a cheque
exam: The bank clerk pointed to the endorsement on the back of the cheque; which meaning of endorsement is this? | A note on a driving licence | Public support for a political party | *A signature on the back of a cheque | A deal where a famous person is paid to promote a product
